  "final_version": "# The Impact of AI on Software Development: A Junior's Guide\n\n## Why AI Tools Matter for New Devs\n\nStarting your first dev job is overwhelming. AI tools can feel like drinking from a firehose. AI coding assistants like Copilot, Cursor, and Claude Code can be your training wheels—helping you write working code faster while you're still learning the patterns. But they're not magic, and knowing when to trust them is a skill itself. The key is learning which tasks to hand off and which to own—let's break that down.\n\n## What AI Handles Well\n\nAs mentioned, the lift comes from knowing where a wrong answer is cheap to catch. The sweet spot: repetitive setup, boilerplate, test stubs, regex, SQL queries, and \"how do I do X in this language\" questions. If Copilot gives you a broken regex, you see it fail immediately. If it writes a test scaffold, you fill in the assertions. This is where you get the biggest lift—maybe 30-50% faster on these specific tasks. But speed on boilerplate isn't the whole story.\n\n## Where You Still Need to Think\n\nThe flip side: AI lacks context you live in every day. Architecture decisions, debugging subtle logic bugs, understanding why a legacy system behaves strangely, and product trade-offs—AI doesn't have your codebase context, your team's conventions, or the business reasoning. If you blindly accept suggestions here, you'll ship bugs that look correct but aren't. That gap is exactly where good habits pay off.\n\n## Building Good Habits\n\nSince AI misses your context, your workflow needs guardrails. Treat AI like a senior dev who types fast but sometimes hallucinates. Always read the diff. Run the tests. Ask \"why does this work?\" before committing. Keep a simple note—date, task, AI suggestion, what you changed, time saved or lost. After two weeks you'll see your own trust calibration. This habit compounds into the career edge we'll cover next.\n\n## Your Career Advantage\n\nThe developers who advance fastest aren't the best prompters—they're the best verifiers. Juniors who learn to drive AI tools well—knowing their limits, verifying output, and focusing human attention on judgment work—will outpace peers who either avoid AI or accept it blindly. The skill isn't prompting; it's verification. Pick one task this week where you'll verify instead of vibe-check, and note the result."
